      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Syringomyelia is an uncommon, poorly understood finding in patients with myotonic dystrophy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We describe a patient with myotonic dystrophy and neck pain in whom an extensive neuroradiologic diagnostic work-up was carried out."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Magnetic resonance imaging revealed a large intramedullary cavity extending from the bulbo-medullary junction to the conus medullaris. After intravenous Gadolinium-DTPA administration, an enhanced nodule was seen at T6. Spinal arteriography showed a single hypervascular nodule and slow flow perimedullary draining veins consistent with hemangioblastoma. After removal of the nodule, a partial collapse of the intramedullary cyst was observed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Intramedullary tumors can underlie syringomyelia in patients with myotonic dystrophy and have to be actively investigated with modern neuroradiologic investigations."
        }
      ]},
